---

[Shares]($link.page('docs/shared-folders')) belong to one device and are
read-only for the others. The swarm folder belongs to the whole swarm: any
device can add files and folders, rename them and delete them, and every other
device sees the change.

# [Using it]($heading.id('using'))

In the app, open **Swarm folder** (on phones, **Swarm** in the navigation
bar). The header shows how many items it holds, how many devices it last synced
with and when. From there:

- **Add file…** picks a file with the system picker and adds it.
- **New folder** creates a folder at the current place in the breadcrumbs.
- Each row can be downloaded, renamed or deleted; tapping a file opens the same
  viewer as browsing a device.
- The **Added by** column (on wide windows) names the device that added each
  file.

On Linux it is also `<mount>/Swarm` in the [mount]($link.page('docs/shared-folders').ref('mount')),
where any program can write to it (see [below]($link.ref('from-the-mount'))).
Without a mount, on every platform, programs reach it through the
[local API]($link.ref('api')).

# [How changes travel]($heading.id('sync'))

The swarm folder's listing is a manifest: every path with its kind, content
hash, size, modification time and author. Each device keeps the whole manifest
on disk, next to its block store, and checks it against every reachable device
every 10 seconds. Only a short fingerprint is exchanged when nothing changed;
the manifest itself travels only when the two differ.

The manifest is a state-based CRDT: two devices that have seen the same edits
hold the same manifest, whatever order the edits arrived in. No device is in
charge and none has to be online for the others to keep working.

File contents are not in the manifest. A file's bytes stay on the device that
added it (and the devices that keep copies, below) and travel when someone opens
it: from the local cache first, then from the devices that hold it, several at
once, most from the fastest ([how it works]($link.page('docs/how-it-works').ref('reading'))).

# [Backup devices]($heading.id('backup'))

A file added from a phone is only readable while that phone is reachable,
unless another device has a copy. A **backup device** keeps a copy of every
file in the swarm folder: give the job to a computer that stays on, such as a
desktop or a home server, and the folder stays readable when the phone is off.

- In the app (Linux desktop): **Node & trust → Back up the swarm
  here**. The card shows how many files are backed up.
- On the command line: `--swarm-backup`, or in the configuration:

```ini
[storage]
swarm_backup = true
```

When the listing shows a new file, the backup device copies it, a few pieces at
a time from whichever devices have it, verifying every piece against its hash;
two backup devices so also back each other up. It remembers what it copied
(`<storage_path>.swarm.json.backup`), so a restart doesn't check everything
again. Other devices need no setting: when the author is offline they read from
the backup devices.

When both the author (e.g. a phone) and a backup device (e.g. a home server)
are online, readers pull chunks from **both** in parallel, letting the faster link
carry most of the load without waiting on slower Wi-Fi or mobile connections
([how it works]($link.page('docs/how-it-works').ref('reading'))).

# [Copies on more devices]($heading.id('copies'))

A backup device keeps everything. To keep each file on a few devices instead,
set how many devices keep each file, once, for the whole swarm:

- In the app: **Node & trust → Keep each swarm file on**: 1 (only where it was
  added) to 4 devices.
- On the command line: `nomadfs --copies 2`.

Each file then lives on the device that added it plus N−1 others, chosen per
file among the devices that accept copies, so the files spread over the devices
instead of all landing on one. Every device works out the same choice on its
own. Phones don't accept copies unless you turn on **Accept copies of other
devices' files** (`accept_copies = true` under `[storage]`); a computer accepts
them unless you turn it off. Backup devices keep everything either way.

The setting is signed by the device that sets it and reaches the others the next
time they meet; the newest one wins.

The copies stay when a file is deleted or replaced: the space they use isn't
reclaimed yet. A file that no reachable device has, because its author has
been offline since adding it, is copied once the author is back.

# [Conflicts]($heading.id('conflicts'))

Every entry carries a version (a Lamport clock and the device that made it) and
remembers the last 8 versions it replaced. When two versions of the same path
meet:

- If one has the other in its history, it is the newer edit and wins.
- If neither does, the edits were made at the same time. **Both are kept**: the
  higher version keeps the name, and the other is renamed to
  `name (conflict from <device>).ext`. Every device computes the same name, so
  the swarm agrees without talking about it.
- A delete never beats a concurrent edit: the edited file stays.
- Deletes are recorded as tombstones, so they replicate like any other edit.

What a device receives from its peers is validated before it is merged: path
length (1024) and depth (32), entry count (100,000) and author names are
bounded, and unsafe paths are refused.

# [Writing from the mount]($heading.id('from-the-mount'))

`<mount>/Swarm` behaves like a normal folder for editors, file managers and the
shell:

- Writes go to a staging file first; reads see them straight away. When the file
  is closed it is chunked and added to the swarm **as one edit**, so other
  devices never see half a write.
- Creating, truncating, appending, `mkdir`, `rm`, `rmdir` (refused while the
  folder is not empty) and `mv` anywhere inside the swarm folder work, including
  an editor's save-to-temporary-then-rename over an existing file.
- A renamed folder keeps its contents visible at the new path at once.
- Moving files between the swarm folder and a device share fails with `EXDEV`
  (a cross-device move); writing to a device share fails with `EROFS`.

Files in `Swarm` show as `0644`, folders as `0755`.

# [From other programs: the local API]($heading.id('api'))

The running node (the app, or `nomadfs` as a service) serves the swarm folder
to the programs of your user over HTTP on `127.0.0.1`, so another app can keep
its data on all your devices with no mount and no server of its own.
[Locket](https://locket.foreachdeveloper.com) keeps its shared secrets vault
this way. It needs NomadFS 0.6.0 or later.

**Finding it.** The node writes `api.json` in its device folder, next to its
keys (under `~/.nomadfs` by default), owner-only, and removes it when it stops:

```json
{ "version": 1, "url": "http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<token>/v1/" }
```

The token is the secret: only your user can read the file. On Linux and macOS
the node also closes connections that come from another user's process. A
client should refuse an `api.json` that other users can read.

**Endpoints**, under that URL:

| Request | Answer |
|---|---|
| `GET status` | `{"synced", "entries", "peers_synced", "last_sync"}` |
| `GET list/<dir>` | `[{"name", "dir", "size", "mtime", "etag"}]`, at most 10,000 |
| `GET files/<path>` | the file, with `ETag: "<content id>"` |
| `PUT files/<path>` | the body becomes the file, 8 MiB at most |
| `DELETE files/<path>` | removes the file |

**Writing without losing edits.** Every file's ETag is its content id.

- `PUT` with `If-Match: "<etag>"` writes only while the file is still that
  version; with `If-None-Match: *`, only while nothing is there. Otherwise the
  answer is `412 Precondition Failed`: read the file again, merge, retry.
- `DELETE` honours `If-Match` the same way.
- Until the node has synced the swarm folder with its peers once since it
  started, writes answer `503`. A device that just woke up holds an empty or
  old folder, and it shouldn't overwrite the swarm's.
- Two devices that write before they meet still produce a
  [conflict copy]($link.ref('conflicts')): look for
  `name (conflict from <device>).ext` and merge it.

Other answers: `404` for a missing file, `413` for a body over 8 MiB, `503`
when a file's data is on no reachable device.

**From the shell**, `nomadfs swarm` is a client of the same API:

```bash
nomadfs swarm status
nomadfs swarm ls notes                            # JSON, with etags
nomadfs swarm get notes/todo.md > todo.md
nomadfs swarm put notes/todo.md todo.md --if-match <etag>
nomadfs swarm put notes/new.md --create < new.md
nomadfs swarm rm notes/old.md --if-match <etag>
```
